  FL: {
    intro: "Florida has the country's most active residential pool-building industry after California — roughly half of Florida single-family homes have a pool or screened pool cage. Construction runs year-round, labor is moderate, and the main cost drivers are pool cages, wind-load engineering, and high-water-table dewatering.",
    permits: "Florida pool permits fall under the Florida Building Code, which includes specific residential swimming pool safety provisions (FBC Residential Section 454). Florida requires at least one of four safety features: a 4-foot barrier fence, approved door/window alarms on every opening facing the pool, a power safety cover, or a pool exit alarm. Miami-Dade and Broward enforce stricter hurricane wind-load standards on pool enclosures (HVHZ — High Velocity Hurricane Zone). Permit fees typically run $1,000–$2,500. Plan review in Miami-Dade and Palm Beach can take 6–10 weeks.",
    climate: "Florida's usable pool season is 9–11 months statewide, and south Florida pools are comfortable with minimal heating year-round. The big seasonal factor is hurricane season (June–November): active storm periods pause construction, drive insurance premiums up, and occasionally damage screened pool cages. Most Florida pool builds include a screened enclosure ('pool cage') that adds $12,000–$45,000 to the total and has its own wind-load and HVHZ requirements.",
    soil: "Florida's soils are overwhelmingly sandy with a high water table — often within 3–6 feet of the surface, especially in coastal and central Florida. Dewatering during excavation is routine, and fiberglass shells are particularly popular here because they install faster than gunite in unstable, wet conditions. Fiberglass shell float-out — where a shell lifts out of the ground from hydrostatic pressure — is a known risk that qualified Florida installers guard against with hydrostatic relief valves and proper backfilling.",
    metros: "Miami-Dade, Broward, and Palm Beach counties are Florida's most expensive pool markets — HVHZ engineering, labor, and permit timelines all add cost. Naples, Marco Island, and Sarasota price similarly due to high-end custom work. Tampa Bay and Orlando are more moderately priced and represent the largest volume of new builds. North Florida (Jacksonville, Pensacola) is the state's most affordable pool market.",
    builders: "Florida has hundreds of licensed pool contractors. The largest names include Coastal Pools (Jacksonville), All Seasons Pools (Orlando), Van Kirk & Sons Pools (South Florida), Rizzetta Pools, Premier Pools & Spas (multiple franchises), Blue Haven Pools, and Water's Edge Pools. The Florida Swimming Pool Association (FSPA) maintains a certified-builder directory and is the primary industry body in the state.",
  },

  GA: {
    intro: "Georgia has a strong, growing residential pool market concentrated in the Atlanta metro. Labor rates are slightly below the national median, permit costs are moderate, and a 7-month uncovered pool season supports the economics of a new build.",
    permits: "Georgia pool permits are issued at the city and county level under the Georgia State Minimum Standard Codes (based on IRC). Cobb, Fulton, Gwinnett, DeKalb, and Forsyth counties are the biggest jurisdictions for metro Atlanta builds. Georgia follows the ICC 48-inch barrier rule, self-closing gates, and separate electrical inspection. Permit fees run $900–$1,800. Some affluent cities in metro Atlanta — Sandy Springs, Brookhaven, Roswell — have design-review boards that add 2–4 weeks.",
    climate: "Atlanta and north Georgia support a usable pool season from early May through late September — 5–5.5 months uncovered. Coastal Georgia (Savannah, Brunswick) gets an extra month on each end due to marine climate moderation. Summer severe-weather cells and occasional hurricane remnants (Helene, Irma) slow construction; most metro-Atlanta builders avoid scheduling shell pours in late August through mid-September.",
    soil: "North Georgia and metro Atlanta sit on heavily weathered saprolite — clay-rich decomposed granite that ranges from firm to surprisingly soft and is frequently mistaken for bedrock. Actual rock outcrops (granite gneiss) occur frequently in north Atlanta suburbs (Sandy Springs, Roswell, Alpharetta) and add $5,000–$20,000 in rock-breaking costs. Coastal Georgia has sandy soils and high water tables similar to Florida. Middle Georgia red clay is expansive and requires standard clay-soil reinforcement.",
    metros: "North Atlanta metro (Alpharetta, Roswell, Milton, East Cobb, Buckhead) is the state's most expensive pool-building submarket — prices run 15–25% above the Georgia median. Athens, Savannah, and Augusta run roughly at or slightly below the state median. Rural Georgia is competitive. Metro Atlanta builder waitlists typically run 5–8 months during peak demand.",
    builders: "Anthony & Sylvan operates a major Atlanta branch and is one of the largest builders in the state. Premier Pools & Spas runs multiple Atlanta franchises. Blue Haven Pools has an Atlanta office. Regional independents include Mirage Pools (Gainesville), Legendary Pools (Alpharetta), Peachtree Pools, and Georgia Pool Builders. The Georgia Aquatic Professionals Association is a useful vendor resource.",
  },

