    soil: "Volcanic soils dominate. Oahu and Maui have a mix of weathered basalt, clay, and coral limestone near shorelines. The Big Island has extensive recent lava flows — in some areas builders must excavate through solid basalt, which requires blasting and can add $30,000–$80,000 to a project. Kauai has deeper red-clay soils. Coral limestone near shorelines requires specialized excavation and steel reinforcement.",
    metros: "Honolulu (Oahu) handles most of Hawaii's pool volume and is the most established market. Maui (Wailea, Kaanapali, Kihei) supports significant high-end custom work with the longest timelines. Big Island and Kauai have limited local crews and often import labor. Freight surcharges on fiberglass shells can exceed $25,000; most Hawaii pools are gunite to avoid the shipping problem entirely.",
    builders: "Hawaii's established builders include Pacific Aquascapes (Oahu), Aloha Pools & Spas, Blue Water Pools (Oahu), Waterworks Hawaii, and Premier Pools & Spas (Oahu franchise). Custom high-end work on Maui and the Big Island is handled by firms like Island Pools & Spas (Maui) and Kona Pools. The Hawaii Pool & Spa Association is the primary local industry body.",
